and Community Engagement and Social Change)","notes":"Select one course from:"}],"label":"Preparation for the Major"}}],"narrative":[],"overrides_applied":[]},"nodes":[{"id":"AN N EA 14W","title":"Medicine, Magic, and Science in Ancient Times","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Preparation for the Major: Medical"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"English Composition 3","requires":{"type":"course","code":"ENGCOMP 3","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour. Not open for credit to students with credit for course 14. Overview of history of medicine and sciences, focusing especially on Ancient Near East, China, and Meso-America. Satisfies Writing II requirement. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ANNEA14W"},{"id":"CESC 100XP","title":"Perspectives on Civic Engagement for Social Justice","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Community Engagement and Social Change"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Seminar, three hours. Community-based learning course. Examination of theories and concepts of civic engagement as means to achieve social justice. Exploration of what informs and influences student theories of change. Reflection on these concepts through work in collaboration with select community-based organizations dedicated to changing status quo with regard to power and opportunity in Los Angeles. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/CESC100XP"},{"id":"CESC 172XP","title":"Community-Engaged Research to Address Health Disparities","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Community Engagement and Social Change"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; fieldwork, three hours. Examination of use of community-engaged research to understand and address health disparities in minority communities. Focus on chronic disease disparities: inequities in risk and outcomes from conditions such as hypertension, diabetes, obesity, heart disease, chronic pulmonary diseases, autoimmune disorders, and HIV. Examination also of role these conditions play in COVID-19 risk and disparities, and role that community-engaged strategies can play in preventing or reducing health disparities. Includes case studies, discussions with community partners and researchers, and assignments to explore impact of community engagement on chronic disease disparities and to mitigate inequitable impact of COVID-19. Student teams partner with community organizations and develop partnered project, with deliverable to community partner due at end of course. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/CESC172XP"},{"id":"CLASSIC 148","title":"Early Greek Medicine and Thought","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Classics"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour. Versions of medical theory and practice in context of Greek intellectual and cultural developments. Readings from medical, philosophical, and historical texts. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/CLASSIC148"},{"id":"COM LIT 1E","title":"Social Media and Storytelling: Comparing Cultures","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Preparation for the Major: Digital"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"satisfaction of Entry-Level Writing requirement","requires":{"type":"condition","kind":"other","text":"satisfaction of Entry-Level Writing requirement"},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, two hours; discussion, one hour. Study of ways in which social media construct increasingly diverse and decentered narratives—with which we make localized sense of world. Equal emphasis on textual, visual, and sonic networks in arts, politics, and health care. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMLIT1E"},{"id":"COM LIT 180","title":"Variable Topics: Health Humanities in Comparative Contexts","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: Comparative Literature"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Seminar, three hours. Designed for juniors/seniors. Study and discussion of defined periods and approaches in health humanities, giving pride of place to literary and cultural expressions in dialogue with other disciplines such as anthropology, history, linguistics, philosophy, psychology, or sociology. Consult Schedule of Classes for topics to be offered in specific term. May be repeated for credit with topic change. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMLIT180"},{"id":"DGT HUM 30","title":"Los Angeles Tech City: Digital Technologies and Spatial Justice","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Preparation for the Major: Digital"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, two and one half hours; studio, two hours. Investigation of spatial justice and injustice in multi-ethnic city of Los Angeles through Lens of three thematic technologies that built and transformed Los Angeles into global metropolis: cars and highways, networking technologies culminating in Internet and World Wide Web, and film and broadcast media. Use of innovative forms of investigation and communication, from digital mapping to video-sensing, to integrate interpretative and historical approaches of humanities with material and projective practices of design. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/DGTHUM30"},{"id":"EE BIOL 17","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Preparation for the Major: Medical"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ELTS 112","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: European Languages and Transcultural Studies"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ELTS 187","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["The Major: Capstone Senior Thesis"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ELTS C101XP","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: European Languages and Transcultural Studies"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ENGCOMP 3","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"not_in_catalog","in_major":false,"role":"prerequisite","groups":[],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ENGL 118E","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: English"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ENGL M30","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Preparation for the Major: Environmental"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"ENGL M118F","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["The Major: English"],"column":